## What is wrong (one paragraph)

The registrations for `POST /api/nft/verify/transaction` (`nft-certificate.openapi.ts:1057`-`:1082`) and `POST /api/nft/verify/asset` (`:1084`-`:1109`) declare their request body as a multi-line `body: {` block whose only key is `content:` (`:1066`, `:1093`), with no `required`, so the published spec says the body is OPTIONAL. The handlers say otherwise: `routes/nft.routes.ts:387` (`router.post('/verify/transaction', ...)`) builds `z.object({ txHash: ... })` (`:389`-`:391`), runs `safeParse(req.body)` (`:393`) and answers 400 on failure; `:421` (`router.post('/verify/asset', ...)`) does the same with `z.object({ policyId, assetNameHex })` (`:423`-`:426`, `:428`). A `z.object` with required keys rejects an absent body, so an absent body is always a 400. KS-1364's sweep 4 measured exactly that: Schemathesis sent no body and got 400 (`/verify/transaction` 2 cases, `/verify/asset` 1 case, `positive_data_acceptance`). The ticket's direction is verbatim: "mark `requestBody.required: true` where the handler's Zod schema rejects an absent body, at the generator (`*.openapi.ts`)". In the multi-line form the house shape is a `required: true,` line inside the `body:` block (`kyc.openapi.ts:474`, `auth.openapi.ts:626`). Both routes are public by product decision (KS-442 comment `:1060`, `:1087`; `security: []` `:1061`, `:1088`); that does not change.

